const e=[{id:"sv-4-001",phase:4,topic:"4.1.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:`A NOC analyst studies **X = engineers on shift** and **Y = average alarm-acknowledgement time (min)** over 12 shifts. The working sums are:

$S_{xx}=400$, $S_{yy}=225$, $S_{xy}=-240$

What is the Pearson correlation coefficient r?`,options:["−0.80","−0.64","−0.60","+0.80"],answer:0,hint:"$r=\\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx}\\,S_{yy}}}$ — the sign of r comes from $S_{xy}$.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$S_{xx}=400$, $S_{yy}=225$, $S_{xy}=-240$, n = 12 shifts."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:`Pearson r divides the co-variation by the square root of both sums of squares:

$$r=\\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx}\\,S_{yy}}}$$`},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$r=\\dfrac{-240}{\\sqrt{400\\times 225}}=\\dfrac{-240}{\\sqrt{90000}}=\\dfrac{-240}{300}=-0.80$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"r = −0.80: a **strong negative** linear relationship. More engineers on shift go with faster acknowledgement. It is still only an association, not proof of cause."}],final:"r = −0.80 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","This is r² (0.80² = 0.64) with the sign kept. r itself is not squared.","This is Sxy ÷ Sxx = −240 ÷ 400, which is the regression slope b₁, not r.","The sign of Sxy was dropped. A negative Sxy always gives a negative r."],trap:"Do not confuse $\\dfrac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}$ (slope) with $\\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx}S_{yy}}}$ (r)."},{id:"sv-4-002",phase:4,topic:"4.1.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A simple regression of **first-call resolution %** (Y) on **average transfers per call** (X) has a **negative slope** and **R-sq = 72.25%**. What is the correlation coefficient r?",options:["−0.85","−0.72","−0.52","+0.85"],answer:0,hint:"In simple regression $R^2=r^2$, so $r=\\pm\\sqrt{R^2}$ with the sign of the slope.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"R-sq = 72.25% = 0.7225 and the slope b₁ is negative."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$r=\\pm\\sqrt{R^2}$$ The sign is taken from the slope."},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$r=-\\sqrt{0.7225}=-0.85$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"A strong negative relationship: more transfers per call go with a lower first-call resolution rate."}],final:"r = −0.85 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","R² (0.7225) was reported as r. In simple regression r is the square root of R².","R² was squared again (0.7225² = 0.522) instead of square-rooted.","The square root is right but the sign is lost. r takes the sign of the slope, which is negative."],trap:"R-sq is always positive, so the sign of r has to come from the slope."},{id:"sv-4-003",phase:4,topic:"4.1.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A Green Belt correlates **backlog age (days)** with **weekly escalations** over **20 weeks** (α = 0.05):

\`\`\`
Correlation: Backlog_Age, Escalations

Pearson correlation    0.430
P-Value                0.058
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is correct?`,options:["There is a significant positive correlation because r is positive","Backlog age has been shown to have no effect on escalations","Backlog age explains 43% of the variation in escalations","Fail to reject H₀: at α = 0.05 there is not enough evidence of a linear correlation"],answer:3,hint:"Correlation test: H₀ ρ = 0. Reject H₀ only when the P-Value ≤ α.",steps:[{title:"Locate the decision number",body:"H₀: ρ = 0 (no linear correlation). Hₐ: ρ ≠ 0. The P-Value is **0.058**."},{title:"Decide",body:"0.058 > 0.05, so **fail to reject H₀**. The data do not show a significant linear correlation at the 5% level."},{title:"Eliminate the look-alikes",body:'A positive r is not evidence by itself. "No effect" overstates a fail-to-reject result. 43% would need r², not r.'}],final:"P = 0.058 > 0.05 → fail to reject H₀ → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The sign and size of r do not decide significance. The decision comes from the P-Value, and 0.058 > 0.05.","Failing to reject H₀ does not prove there is no relationship. It only means the evidence is not strong enough with 20 weeks of data.","r was read as R². The share of variation explained is r² = 0.43² ≈ 18.5%, and it is not significant here anyway.",""],trap:'Never write "accept H₀". A borderline p-value may justify collecting more data.'},{id:"sv-4-004",phase:4,topic:"4.1.1",qtype:"table",difficulty:3,stem:`Four candidate X’s were correlated with **MTTR (h)** for P2 incidents. Site distance was only recorded for a few incidents.

| Candidate X | n | Pearson r | P-Value |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Engineer tenure (months) | 30 | −0.82 | 0.000 |
| Ticket reassignments | 30 | 0.64 | 0.000 |
| Site distance (km) | 4 | 0.86 | 0.140 |
| Shift start hour | 30 | 0.12 | 0.528 |

At α = 0.05, which X has the **strongest statistically significant** linear relationship with MTTR?`,options:["Ticket reassignments","Site distance","Shift start hour","Engineer tenure"],answer:3,hint:"Screen on P-Value ≤ α first, then compare strength by |r| (the sign gives only the direction).",steps:[{title:"Keep only significant X’s",body:"P ≤ 0.05: Engineer tenure (0.000) and Ticket reassignments (0.000). Site distance (0.140) and Shift start hour (0.528) drop out."},{title:"Compare strength by |r|",body:"|−0.82| = 0.82 > |0.64| = 0.64, so tenure has the stronger linear relationship. The minus sign means MTTR falls as tenure rises."},{title:"Watch the small sample",body:"Site distance looks strongest (0.86), but four points cannot rule out chance. It is a candidate for more data collection, not a conclusion."}],final:"Engineer tenure: |r| = 0.82, P = 0.000 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["It is significant, but |r| = 0.64 is weaker than |−0.82|. Strength is judged by |r|, so a negative r is not weaker.","It has the largest |r|, but with only n = 4 the P-Value is 0.140 > 0.05, so the correlation is not significant.","r = 0.12 is weak and P = 0.528 > 0.05. There is no evidence of a linear relationship.",""],trap:"A large r from a tiny sample can still be non-significant."},{id:"sv-4-005",phase:4,topic:"4.1.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following statements about the Pearson correlation coefficient r are true **EXCEPT**:",options:["r measures the strength and direction of a linear relationship","r has no units and always lies between −1 and +1","r can be close to 0 even when X and Y have a strong curved relationship","A strong r between X and Y proves that changing X will change Y"],answer:3,hint:"Correlation measures linear association only. It never proves cause and effect.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"r summarises how closely the points follow a straight line, and in which direction. It says nothing about why."},{title:"Apply to the options",body:"Three options describe r correctly. The odd one out claims causation. A lurking variable, reverse cause or coincidence can all produce a strong r."},{title:"Conclude",body:"Cause and effect needs a controlled change, such as a pilot or designed experiment, not historical correlation."}],final:"Correlation ≠ causation → answer **D**",whyWrong:["This is true. It is the definition of r.","This is true. r is standardised by both standard deviations.","This is true. r only measures straight-line association, so a U-shape can give r ≈ 0.",""]},{id:"sv-4-006",phase:4,topic:"4.1.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"If two variables have a Pearson correlation of **r = 0**, they have no relationship of any kind. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Pearson r detects straight-line relationships only.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Pearson r measures linear association."},{title:"Apply",body:"Y = (X − 5)² for X = 0 … 10 is a perfect relationship, but it is symmetric, so r = 0. Always look at the scatter plot as well as r."}],final:"r = 0 means no linear relationship only → **False** (answer **B**)",whyWrong:["r = 0 only rules out a **linear** relationship. A curved (for example U-shaped) relationship can still be strong.",""]},{id:"sv-4-007",phase:4,topic:"4.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Over 18 months, a team finds **r = 0.91** (p < 0.001) between the **number of marketing SMS sent** per month and the **number of billing complaints** per month. A manager proposes cutting the SMS campaigns to reduce complaints. What is the BEST response?",options:["Cut the SMS campaigns now, because an r above 0.80 proves cause and effect","Ignore the result, because r must be above 0.95 to be meaningful","Conclude that 83% of complaints are caused by SMS, because r² = 0.83","Treat it as an association only: look for a lurking variable such as subscriber-base growth, and test the change in a controlled pilot before acting"],answer:3,hint:"Correlation shows association, not cause. Look for lurking variables and confirm with a controlled change.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Historical correlation cannot separate cause from coincidence or from a third variable driving both series."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Subscriber growth is a likely common driver: more customers means more SMS and more bills, so more complaints. Normalising per 1,000 customers or running a pilot would test the claim."},{title:"Eliminate",body:'Acting at once assumes causation. Dismissing r = 0.91 wastes a lead. r² is not "percent caused".'}],final:"Association ≠ causation → answer **D**",whyWrong:["No size of r proves causation. Both counts may simply grow with the number of subscribers.","r = 0.91 with p < 0.001 is a strong, significant association. It is worth investigating, not ignoring.","r² is the share of variation explained by the linear fit. It is not the share of complaints caused by X.",""]},{id:"sv-4-008",phase:4,topic:"4.1.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:`For **n = 10** business orders, X = **number of services in the order** and Y = **provisioning time (h)**:

$\\bar{x}=5$, $\\bar{y}=20$, $S_{xx}=60$, $S_{xy}=84$, $S_{yy}=144$

What is the intercept b₀ of the least-squares line?`,options:["13.00","16.43","17.08","27.00"],answer:0,hint:"$b_1=\\dfrac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}$, then $b_0=\\bar{y}-b_1\\bar{x}$.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$\\bar{x}=5$, $\\bar{y}=20$, $S_{xx}=60$, $S_{xy}=84$. $S_{yy}$ is not needed for the line."},{title:"Find the slope first",body:"$$b_1=\\dfrac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}=\\dfrac{84}{60}=1.4$$"},{title:"Find the intercept",body:"$$b_0=\\bar{y}-b_1\\bar{x}=20-1.4\\times 5=20-7=13.0$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Ŷ = 13.0 + 1.4X: about 13 h of fixed work per order plus 1.4 h for each extra service. The line always passes through (x̄, ȳ) = (5, 20)."}],final:"b₀ = 13.00 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The slope was inverted (Sxx ÷ Sxy = 0.714), giving b₀ = 20 − 0.714 × 5.","The slope used Syy instead of Sxx (84 ÷ 144 = 0.583), giving b₀ = 20 − 0.583 × 5.","b₁x̄ was added to ȳ instead of subtracted (20 + 7)."],trap:"Syy appears in r, never in the slope."},{id:"sv-4-009",phase:4,topic:"4.1.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"For 15 field-repair jobs, X = **technician experience (years)** and Y = **repair time (h)**: $S_{xx}=150$, $S_{yy}=30$, $S_{xy}=-45$. What is the slope b₁?",options:["−1.50","−0.67","−0.30","+0.30"],answer:2,hint:"$b_1=\\dfrac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"$S_{xx}=150$, $S_{xy}=-45$."},{title:"Pick the formula and substitute",body:"$$b_1=\\dfrac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}=\\dfrac{-45}{150}=-0.30$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Each extra year of experience goes with about 0.30 h (18 min) less repair time on average."}],final:"b₁ = −0.30 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Syy was used in the denominator (−45 ÷ 30). The slope divides by Sxx.","This is the correlation r = −45 ÷ √(150 × 30), not the slope.","","The size is right but the sign of Sxy was dropped."]},{id:"sv-4-010",phase:4,topic:"4.1.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:`In a retail store, **SIM-swap time (min)** is modelled on **customers waiting in the queue** (X, observed range 5–60):

**Ŷ = 4.2 + 0.35X**

What is the predicted SIM-swap time when 40 customers are waiting?`,options:["14.0","18.2","168.4","182.0"],answer:1,hint:"$\\hat{Y}=b_0+b_1X$, used only inside the X range of the data.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"b₀ = 4.2, b₁ = 0.35, X = 40 (inside the 5–60 range, so no extrapolation)."},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$\\hat{Y}=b_0+b_1X=4.2+0.35\\times 40=4.2+14.0=18.2$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"With 40 customers waiting, the average SIM swap is predicted to take about 18.2 minutes."}],final:"Ŷ = 18.2 min → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The intercept 4.2 was left out (only 0.35 × 40).","","The intercept was multiplied by X and the slope added (4.2 × 40 + 0.35).","The two coefficients were added first and then multiplied by X ((4.2 + 0.35) × 40)."]},{id:"sv-4-011",phase:4,topic:"4.1.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"The ANOVA table of a simple regression of **dispute-resolution days** on **invoice lines** shows **SS Regression = 540**, **SS Error = 135** and **SS Total = 675**. What is R-sq?",options:["20.0%","25.0%","80.0%","89.4%"],answer:2,hint:"$R^2=\\dfrac{SS_{Regression}}{SS_{Total}}=1-\\dfrac{SS_{Error}}{SS_{Total}}$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"SS Regression = 540, SS Error = 135, SS Total = 675 (540 + 135 = 675 ✓)."},{title:"Pick the formula and substitute",body:"$$R^2=\\dfrac{SS_{Regression}}{SS_{Total}}=\\dfrac{540}{675}=0.80$$"},{title:"Check another way",body:"$$R^2=1-\\dfrac{SS_{Error}}{SS_{Total}}=1-\\dfrac{135}{675}=1-0.20=0.80$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"80% of the variation in resolution days is explained by the linear relationship with invoice lines."}],final:"R-sq = 80.0% → answer **C**",whyWrong:["This is SS Error ÷ SS Total, the share of variation **not** explained.","SS Error was divided by SS Regression. R-sq uses SS Total as the denominator.","","This is √0.80 = r, the correlation, not R-sq."]},{id:"sv-4-012",phase:4,topic:"4.1.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"A simple linear regression of **page-load time (s)** on **concurrent users** is fitted to **n = 20** observations and gives **SS Error = 72**. What is the standard error of the regression, S?",options:["1.90","2.00","3.60","4.00"],answer:1,hint:"$S=\\sqrt{MSE}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{SS_{Error}}{n-2}}$ for one predictor.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"n = 20, one predictor, SS Error = 72."},{title:"Find the error degrees of freedom",body:"DF Error = n − 2 = 18 (one df for the slope and one for the constant)."},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$S=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{SS_{Error}}{n-2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{72}{18}}=\\sqrt{4}=2.00$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"S = 2.00 s is the typical distance of an observed page-load time from the fitted line, in the units of Y. Smaller is better."}],final:"S = 2.00 s → answer **B**",whyWrong:["n was used instead of the error degrees of freedom n − 2 (√(72 ÷ 20)).","","SSE was divided by n and not square-rooted (72 ÷ 20).","This is MSE = 72 ÷ 18. S is the square root of MSE."],trap:"MSE is in squared units; S is back in the units of Y."},{id:"sv-4-013",phase:4,topic:"4.1.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Minitab output for **ticket resolution time (min)** against **number of reassignments** (data range 0–6 reassignments, n = 27):

\`\`\`
Regression Analysis: Resolve_min versus Reassignments

Regression Equation
Resolve_min = 22.60 + 7.15 Reassignments

Coefficients
Term            Coef  SE Coef  T-Value  P-Value
Constant       22.60     2.10    10.76    0.000
Reassignments   7.15     0.62    11.53    0.000

Model Summary
      S    R-sq  R-sq(adj)
   5.84  84.4%     83.8%
\`\`\`

What is the predicted resolution time for a ticket with **4 reassignments**?`,options:["4.58 min","28.60 min","51.20 min","56.88 min"],answer:2,hint:"Read the Coef column: Ŷ = Constant + Coef × X.",steps:[{title:"Locate the numbers",body:"Coef column: Constant = 22.60, Reassignments = 7.15. X = 4 is inside the 0–6 range."},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$\\hat{Y}=22.60+7.15\\times 4=22.60+28.60=51.20$$"},{title:"Check the model is usable",body:"The slope P-Value is 0.000 and R-sq = 84.4%, so the prediction is meaningful. S = 5.84 min shows the typical scatter around it."}],final:"Ŷ = 51.20 min → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The SE Coef column was used (2.10 + 0.62 × 4) instead of the Coef column.","The constant 22.60 was left out (7.15 × 4 only).","","The T-Value column was used (10.76 + 11.53 × 4) instead of the coefficients."],trap:"SE Coef and T-Value sit next to Coef. Only Coef goes into the equation."},{id:"sv-4-014",phase:4,topic:"4.1.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A Green Belt regresses **upsell conversion %** on **call length (min)** for 20 agents (α = 0.05):

\`\`\`
Analysis of Variance
Source       DF   Adj SS   Adj MS   F-Value  P-Value
Regression    1    38.40    38.40      2.62    0.123
Error        18   264.00    14.67
Total        19   302.40

Model Summary
      S    R-sq  R-sq(adj)
   3.83  12.7%      7.8%
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is correct?`,options:["Reject H₀, because the F-Value is greater than 1","Fail to reject H₀: β₁ = 0; call length is not a significant linear predictor, so look for other X’s","The model is useful, because it explains 87.3% of the variation in conversion","Accept H₀: call length has been proven to have no effect on conversion"],answer:1,hint:"Regression ANOVA: H₀ β₁ = 0. Reject H₀ only if the P-Value ≤ α.",steps:[{title:"Locate the decision number",body:"Regression row: F = 2.62, P-Value = **0.123**."},{title:"Decide",body:"0.123 > 0.05, so **fail to reject H₀**. There is no significant evidence that the slope differs from zero. R-sq = 12.7% confirms that call length explains little."},{title:"Next step",body:"Go back to the X-list (fishbone or C&E matrix) and test other candidate X’s."}],final:"P = 0.123 > 0.05 → fail to reject H₀ → answer **B**",whyWrong:["F is judged by its P-Value (or a critical F of about 4.41 here), not by being above 1. P = 0.123 > 0.05.","","87.3% is 1 − R-sq, the share **not** explained. The model explains only 12.7%.",'Exam wording is "fail to reject", and a non-significant result does not prove there is no effect.']},{id:"sv-4-015",phase:4,topic:"4.1.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Part of a regression ANOVA for **change success rate** on **test-environment hours** (n = 17) is shown with blanks:

\`\`\`
Analysis of Variance
Source       DF   Adj SS   Adj MS   F-Value
Regression    1    250.0      ***       ***
Error        15    150.0      ***
Total        16    400.0
\`\`\`

What is the F-Value?`,options:["1.67","10.00","25.00","26.67"],answer:2,hint:"$MS=\\dfrac{SS}{DF}$ and $F=\\dfrac{MS_{Regression}}{MS_{Error}}$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"SS Regression = 250 on 1 DF; SS Error = 150 on 15 DF."},{title:"Find the mean squares",body:"$$MS_{Regression}=\\dfrac{250}{1}=250$$ $$MS_{Error}=\\dfrac{150}{15}=10$$"},{title:"Calculate F",body:"$$F=\\dfrac{MS_{Regression}}{MS_{Error}}=\\dfrac{250}{10}=25.00$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"F = 25 on (1, 15) DF is far above the 5% critical value (about 4.54), so the slope is significant. R-sq is $\\dfrac{250}{400}=0.625$, or 62.5%."}],final:"F = 25.00 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The SS values were divided (250 ÷ 150) instead of the mean squares.","MS Regression was divided by the Total mean square (400 ÷ 16 = 25) instead of MS Error.","","MS Error used n − 1 = 16 as its df (150 ÷ 16) instead of the Error DF of 15."],trap:"F is a ratio of mean squares, never of sums of squares."},{id:"sv-4-016",phase:4,topic:"4.1.2",qtype:"table",difficulty:3,stem:`A contact centre records five half-hour intervals:

| Agents on shift (X) | 4 | 6 | 8 | 10 | 12 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Average wait (s) (Y) | 70 | 58 | 50 | 38 | 34 |

Which least-squares regression equation fits these data?`,options:["$\\hat{Y}=86.8-4.6X$","$\\hat{Y}=13.2-4.6X$","$\\hat{Y}=50.0-4.6X$","$\\hat{Y}=86.8+4.6X$"],answer:0,hint:"$b_1=\\dfrac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}$ and $b_0=\\bar{y}-b_1\\bar{x}$",steps:[{title:"Find the means",body:"$$\\bar{x}=\\dfrac{4+6+8+10+12}{5}=\\dfrac{40}{5}=8$$ $$\\bar{y}=\\dfrac{70+58+50+38+34}{5}=\\dfrac{250}{5}=50$$"},{title:"Find the deviations",body:"x − x̄: −4, −2, 0, 2, 4. y − ȳ: 20, 8, 0, −12, −16."},{title:"Sums of squares and products",body:"$$S_{xx}=16+4+0+4+16=40$$ $$S_{xy}=(-4)(20)+(-2)(8)+(0)(0)+(2)(-12)+(4)(-16)=-184$$"},{title:"Slope and intercept",body:"$$b_1=\\dfrac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}=\\dfrac{-184}{40}=-4.6$$ $$b_0=\\bar{y}-b_1\\bar{x}=50-(-4.6)\\times 8=50+36.8=86.8$$"},{title:"Write the equation",body:"Ŷ = 86.8 − 4.6X: each extra agent goes with about 4.6 s less average wait, within 4–12 agents."}],final:"Ŷ = 86.8 − 4.6X → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","b₁x̄ was added instead of subtracted when finding the intercept: 50 + (−4.6)(8) = 13.2.","ȳ was used as the intercept. b₀ is the predicted Y at X = 0, not the mean of Y.","The intercept is right but the minus sign of the slope was dropped. Wait falls as agents rise, so b₁ must be negative."],trap:"Subtracting a negative b₁x̄ adds to ȳ."},{id:"sv-4-017",phase:4,topic:"4.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"From 24 months of data (price increases of 0–10%), a Green Belt fits **Churn % = 1.9 + 0.42 × Price increase %**. Which statement correctly interprets the coefficient **0.42**?",options:["For each extra 1 percentage point of price increase, average churn is about 0.42 percentage points higher, within the 0–10% range","Churn is 0.42% when there is no price increase","42% of the variation in churn is explained by price increase","Each 1% price rise forces 0.42% of customers to leave"],answer:0,hint:"Slope b₁ = change in average Ŷ per one-unit increase in X, inside the data range.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In Ŷ = b₀ + b₁X the slope b₁ is the change in the predicted Y for a one-unit increase in X."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"X and Y are both in percent, so the slope is +0.42 churn points per 1 point of price increase. It holds only inside the 0–10% data range."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Intercept (1.9) ≠ slope. R-sq ≠ slope. Association ≠ cause."}],final:"b₁ = +0.42 points of churn per point of price increase → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","That describes the intercept, and the intercept here is 1.9, not 0.42.","That is the meaning of R-sq, which is a separate output. The slope is not a percentage of variation.",'Historical regression shows association, not cause. "Forces" claims causation the data cannot prove.']},{id:"sv-4-018",phase:4,topic:"4.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A model of **page-load time** on **concurrent sessions** was fitted with sessions between **20 and 80** (R-sq = 95%). Capacity planning asks for the predicted load time at **200 sessions** for a launch event. What should the Green Belt do?",options:["Use the equation, because R-sq = 95% guarantees accuracy at any X","Avoid using the equation at 200 sessions; collect data (for example a load test) near that level first","Use the equation if the residual plots look random","Use the equation and report the 95% prediction interval, which allows for extrapolation"],answer:1,hint:"Use a regression equation only inside the range of X used to build it (no extrapolation).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A regression equation is only supported inside the X range of the data. Outside it the relationship may curve, for example when servers saturate."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"200 is 2.5 times the largest observed X (80). That is heavy extrapolation."},{title:"Recommend",body:"Run a load test or gather data at high session counts, then refit before predicting."}],final:"Do not extrapolate → answer **B**",whyWrong:["R-sq describes fit inside the observed range only. It says nothing about whether the line still holds at 200.","","Good residual plots validate the model inside 20–80 sessions. They cannot show what happens at 200.","Intervals assume the straight line still holds. They do not protect against a relationship that bends outside the data."]},{id:"sv-4-019",phase:4,topic:"4.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"The **least-squares** regression line is the line that:",options:["makes the sum of the residuals equal to zero","minimises the sum of the perpendicular distances from the points to the line","passes through as many of the data points as possible","minimises the sum of the squared vertical distances between the observed Y values and the line"],answer:3,hint:"Least squares = choose b₀ and b₁ to make Σ(y − ŷ)² as small as possible.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each residual is the vertical gap y − ŷ. Least squares picks the line that makes the **sum of squared residuals** (SSE) as small as possible."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Zero-sum residuals are a consequence, not the rule. Perpendicular distance is a different method. Hitting points is not a goal."}],final:"Minimise Σ(y − ŷ)² → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The residuals of the least-squares line do sum to zero, but many other lines through (x̄, ȳ) do too. It is a property, not the fitting criterion.","Perpendicular distances belong to orthogonal regression. Least squares uses vertical distances in Y.","The line usually passes through none of the points. It balances all of them.",""]},{id:"sv-4-020",phase:4,topic:"4.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In a simple regression Y = f(X), which statement about X and Y is correct?",options:["X is the dependent (response) variable and Y is the independent (predictor) variable","Both X and Y must be discrete counts","X must be normally distributed for the regression to be valid","X is the independent (input or predictor) variable and Y is the dependent (output or response) variable"],answer:3,hint:"Y = f(X): X is the input (independent), Y is the output (dependent).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In Y = f(X), Y is the process output or CTQ and X is the input that is thought to drive it."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Reversed roles, the data-type requirement and normal X are all wrong. The normality assumption is about the residuals."}],final:"X = independent, Y = dependent → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The roles are reversed. Y is the output that depends on X.","Regression is built for a continuous Y. X is usually continuous too.","Normality is required of the residuals, not of X.",""]},{id:"sv-4-021",phase:4,topic:"4.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A simple regression of **repair cost** on **device age** gives **R-sq = 64%**. Which statement BEST describes this value?",options:["64% of the variation in repair cost is explained by the linear relationship with device age","The correlation between repair cost and device age is 0.64","64% of repair costs will be predicted to within ±S of the actual value","Device age causes 64% of the repair cost"],answer:0,hint:"$R^2=\\dfrac{SS_{Regression}}{SS_{Total}}$ = share of the variation in Y explained by the model.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"R-sq compares the variation explained by the fitted line with the total variation in Y."},{title:"Apply",body:"64% of the variation in repair cost is accounted for by device age. The other 36% is left in the residuals."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"R-sq is not r, not a prediction-accuracy rate and not a causal share."}],final:"R-sq = share of variation in Y explained → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","r = √0.64 = ±0.80. R-sq is r², not r.","R-sq says nothing about the share of predictions inside a band. That is a property of S and the intervals.","R-sq measures explained variation from historical data. It does not split cost into causes."]},{id:"sv-4-022",phase:4,topic:"4.1.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`A Green Belt wants to model **delivery lead time (Y)** on **order size (X)**. Put these steps in the correct order:

- **P** — Fit the regression and check the slope P-Value and R-sq
- **Q** — Draw a scatter plot and calculate r
- **R** — Use the equation to predict Y within the observed X range
- **S** — Check the residual plots`,options:["P → Q → S → R","Q → P → S → R","Q → P → R → S","Q → S → P → R"],answer:1,hint:"Look (scatter, r) → fit → validate (residuals) → use (predict).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Regression follows look → fit → check → use."},{title:"Apply",body:"Q (scatter plot and r) shows whether a straight line is sensible. P fits the line and tests the slope. S checks the residual assumptions. Only then R: predict, inside the X range."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Any order that predicts before validating, or checks residuals before fitting, is wrong."}],final:"Q → P → S → R → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The model is fitted before the scatter plot is drawn, so curvature or outliers could be missed before fitting.","","The equation is used to predict before the residuals have validated the model.","Residuals do not exist until the model has been fitted, so they cannot be checked before P."]},{id:"sv-4-023",phase:4,topic:"4.1.2",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"In simple linear regression, the slope b₁ always has the same sign as the correlation coefficient r. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"$b_1=\\dfrac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}$ and $r=\\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx}S_{yy}}}$ share the numerator $S_{xy}$.",steps:[{title:"Compare the formulas",body:"$$b_1=\\dfrac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}}$$ $$r=\\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx}\\,S_{yy}}}$$ Both denominators are positive."},{title:"Conclude",body:"The sign of both depends only on Sxy, so a negative slope always means a negative r, and a positive slope a positive r."}],final:"Same sign always → **True** (answer **A**)",whyWrong:["","Both b₁ and r have Sxy in the numerator and a positive denominator, so their signs always match."]},{id:"sv-4-024",phase:4,topic:"4.1.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A model of **fibre-install time (h)** on **route length (km)** is **Ŷ = 12.5 + 2.4X**. One job had a route of **6 km** and took **25 h**. What is the residual for this job?",options:["−1.9 h","+1.9 h","10.6 h","26.9 h"],answer:0,hint:"$e=Y-\\hat{Y}$ (observed minus fitted), with $\\hat{Y}=b_0+b_1X$.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"b₀ = 12.5, b₁ = 2.4, X = 6 km, observed Y = 25 h."},{title:"Find the fitted value",body:"$$\\hat{Y}=12.5+2.4\\times 6=12.5+14.4=26.9$$"},{title:"Find the residual",body:"$$e=Y-\\hat{Y}=25-26.9=-1.9$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The negative residual means the job was 1.9 h **faster** than the model predicted, so the model over-predicted this job."}],final:"e = −1.9 h → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The residual was calculated as Ŷ − Y. The definition is observed minus fitted, Y − Ŷ.","The intercept was left out of Ŷ (25 − 2.4 × 6 = 25 − 14.4).","This is the fitted value Ŷ, not the residual."],trap:"Residual = observed − fitted. A negative residual means the point lies below the line."},{id:"sv-4-025",phase:4,topic:"4.1.3",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"For a model of **monthly site-visit hours**, one site has observed **Y = 88.0 h** and fitted **Ŷ = 82.6 h**. The model has **S = 2.0 h**. Using the approximation $\\dfrac{e}{S}$ for the standardized residual, what is its value, and is the point flagged as a large residual (|value| > 2)?",options:["−2.70, flagged","1.35, not flagged","2.70, flagged","5.40, flagged"],answer:2,hint:"$e=Y-\\hat{Y}$, standardized residual $\\approx\\dfrac{e}{S}$; Minitab flags |standardized residual| > 2 with R.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Y = 88.0, Ŷ = 82.6, S = 2.0."},{title:"Find the residual",body:"$$e=Y-\\hat{Y}=88.0-82.6=5.4$$"},{title:"Standardise",body:"$$\\dfrac{e}{S}=\\dfrac{5.4}{2.0}=2.70$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"|2.70| > 2, so Minitab would list the site as a large residual (R). Investigate it for a data error or a special cause before deciding what to do."}],final:"Standardized residual ≈ 2.70 > 2 → flagged → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The residual was taken as Ŷ − Y, so the sign is reversed. The point lies above the line, so its residual is positive.","The residual was divided by S² = 4 (the variance) instead of S.","","5.4 is the raw residual in hours. It was not standardised by S."]},{id:"sv-4-026",phase:4,topic:"4.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"For a model of **repair cost** on **device age**, the **residuals-versus-fits** plot shows residuals tightly packed at low fitted values and spreading wider and wider as the fitted value increases (a funnel opening to the right). What does this MOST likely indicate, and what should the team consider?",options:["A missing squared term; add X² to the model","Non-constant variance; consider transforming Y (for example log or Box-Cox) and refitting","The residuals are not independent; check the data collection order","A single outlier; delete the largest residual and refit"],answer:1,hint:"Funnel in residuals vs fits = non-constant variance. Curve = missing term. Trend in order plot = dependence.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Residuals versus fits should be a random, even band around zero. A widening funnel means the variance grows with the fitted value (heteroscedasticity)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Costly repairs vary more than cheap ones. A transformation of Y, such as log, often stabilises the spread. Refit and re-check the plot."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"Curvature, time order and a single outlier each leave a different signature."}],final:"Funnel → non-constant variance → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A missing curvature term shows as a curved (U or arch) band, not a widening spread.","","Dependence is judged on the residuals-versus-order plot as trends or cycles over time.","A funnel is a systematic pattern across the whole range, not one stray point. Deleting data would hide the problem."]},{id:"sv-4-027",phase:4,topic:"4.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Data on **daily call-abandon rate** were collected in time order over 8 weeks. After regressing abandon rate on staffed agents, the **residuals-versus-order** plot shows a repeating pattern: several days above zero, then several below, cycling every week. Which assumption is in doubt?",options:["Normality of the residuals","Constant variance of the residuals","Linearity in X; a quadratic term is needed","Independence of the residuals; a time-related factor such as day of week may be missing from the model"],answer:3,hint:"Residuals vs order: trends, runs or cycles mean the residuals are not independent.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The residuals-versus-order plot checks independence. It should look like random scatter with no runs or cycles."},{title:"Apply",body:"A weekly cycle suggests a day-of-week effect the model ignores. Add it as an X (for example an indicator for weekend) or investigate the cause."}],final:"Weekly cycle in order plot → independence violated → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Normality is judged from the normal probability plot or histogram, not from a pattern over time.","Constant variance is judged from the spread in residuals versus fits.","Missing curvature shows as a curve in residuals versus fits or versus X, not as cycles in time order.",""]},{id:"sv-4-028",phase:4,topic:"4.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In Minitab’s four-in-one residual plots, what is the **normal probability plot of the residuals** mainly used to check?",options:["Whether the residuals are approximately normally distributed","Whether the residuals have constant variance","Whether the residuals are independent over time","Whether the relationship between X and Y is linear"],answer:0,hint:"Each residual plot answers one question: normal plot → normality; vs fits → variance and curvature; vs order → independence.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Points on the normal probability plot that follow a straight line show approximately normal residuals. S-shapes or curved tails show non-normality."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"The other three options belong to the residuals-versus-fits and residuals-versus-order plots."}],final:"Normal probability plot → normality → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Constant variance is checked on the residuals-versus-fits plot.","Independence is checked on the residuals-versus-order plot.","Linearity is checked on the scatter plot or the residuals-versus-fits plot."]},{id:"sv-4-029",phase:4,topic:"4.1.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Each of the following is an assumption about the **residuals** of a least-squares regression model **EXCEPT**:",options:["The X values must follow a normal distribution","The residuals are approximately normally distributed","The residuals have constant variance across the fitted values","The residuals are independent of each other"],answer:0,hint:"The assumptions are about the residuals: normal, constant variance, independent, mean zero. X is not required to be normal.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Least-squares inference needs residuals that are normal, have constant variance and are independent."},{title:"Apply",body:"Nothing is assumed about the distribution of X. X values are often chosen or spread on purpose."}],final:"Normal X is NOT an assumption → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","This is a standard residual assumption. It is checked with the normal probability plot.","This is a standard residual assumption. It is checked with residuals versus fits.","This is a standard residual assumption. It is checked with residuals versus order."]},{id:"sv-4-030",phase:4,topic:"4.1.3",qtype:"table",difficulty:3,stem:`Part of Minitab’s **Fits and Diagnostics** for a model of **ticket backlog (h)** (S = 2.0):

| Obs | Y | Fit | Resid | Std Resid |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 3 | 41.2 | 40.5 | 0.7 | 0.36 |
| 9 | 47.9 | 43.1 | 4.8 | 2.46 R |
| 14 | 36.0 | 37.8 | −1.8 | −0.93 |
| 21 | 52.4 | 51.6 | 0.8 | 0.42 |

Which observation should the team investigate first, and why?`,options:["Obs 21, because it has the largest fitted value","Obs 9, because its standardized residual is above 2 and Minitab flags it with R","Obs 14, because its negative residual means the model badly over-predicts it","None of them, because every raw residual is under 5 h"],answer:1,hint:"Judge residuals on the standardized scale: |Std Resid| > 2 is flagged as a large residual (R).",steps:[{title:"Locate the column",body:"Use **Std Resid**, which puts all residuals on a common scale."},{title:"Apply the rule",body:"Only Obs 9 has |Std Resid| > 2 (2.46), and Minitab marks it R."},{title:"Next action",body:"Check Obs 9 for a recording error or special cause. Do not delete it without a reason."}],final:"Obs 9: Std Resid 2.46 > 2 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["A large fitted value is not a problem. What matters is how far the observation is from its fit.","","The sign shows direction only. |−0.93| is well inside ±2.","Raw residuals must be judged against the model’s scatter. 4.8 h is about 2.5 standard errors."]},{id:"sv-4-031",phase:4,topic:"4.2.1",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:`A quadratic model of **onboarding satisfaction score** on **onboarding-call length X (min)** (data 5–35 min) is

**Ŷ = 20 + 1.5X − 0.04X²**

What is the predicted score for a **25-minute** call?`,options:["12.5","32.5","56.5","82.5"],answer:1,hint:"$\\hat{Y}=b_0+b_1X+b_2X^2$ — square X before multiplying by b₂, and keep every sign.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"b₀ = 20, b₁ = 1.5, b₂ = −0.04, X = 25 (inside 5–35)."},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\hat{Y}=20+1.5\\times 25-0.04\\times 25^2$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\hat{Y}=20+37.5-0.04\\times 625=20+37.5-25=32.5$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"The negative X² term makes the curve bend down. Satisfaction rises with call length, levels off and then falls."}],final:"Ŷ = 32.5 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The constant 20 was left out (37.5 − 25).","","X was not squared in the last term (0.04 × 25 = 1 instead of 0.04 × 625 = 25).","The minus sign on the X² term was ignored (20 + 37.5 + 25)."]},{id:"sv-4-032",phase:4,topic:"4.2.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A straight-line fit of **latency (ms)** on **link utilisation (%)** gave R-sq(adj) = 79.7%. The quadratic fit on the same 24 points gives:

\`\`\`
Polynomial Regression Analysis: Latency_ms versus Utilisation

Latency_ms = 31.8 - 0.62 Utilisation + 0.0118 Utilisation^2

S = 2.20   R-sq = 95.5%   R-sq(adj) = 95.1%

Sequential Analysis of Variance
Source     DF       SS       F      P
Linear      1   1820.4    91.1  0.000
Quadratic   1    337.9    69.8  0.000
\`\`\`

What should the team conclude (α = 0.05)?`,options:["Keep the straight line, because its P-Value is also 0.000","Use the quadratic model, because R-sq always increases when terms are added","Fit a cubic next, because a higher-order model is always better","Use the quadratic model: the quadratic term is significant (P = 0.000) and R-sq(adj) rises from 79.7% to 95.1%"],answer:3,hint:"Keep a higher-order term only if its sequential P-Value ≤ α and R-sq(adj) (or S) improves.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Sequential ANOVA, Quadratic row: P = **0.000**. Model summary: R-sq(adj) **95.1%** against 79.7% for the line, and S = 2.20."},{title:"Decide",body:"The X² term is significant and R-sq(adj), which penalises extra terms, jumps by about 15 points. Keep the quadratic."},{title:"Check",body:"Confirm with the residual plots, and use the curve only inside the observed utilisation range."}],final:"Quadratic term significant, R-sq(adj) 95.1% → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A significant linear term does not mean the line is adequate. The quadratic term adds significant explanation on top of it.","R-sq always rises with an extra term, so the rise alone is no evidence. The decision should rest on the term’s P-Value and R-sq(adj).","Add the next order only if there is evidence such as residual curvature. Extra terms risk overfitting.",""],trap:"Compare models with R-sq(adj), not R-sq."},{id:"sv-4-033",phase:4,topic:"4.2.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"After fitting a straight line of **queue wait** on **agent occupancy**, the residuals-versus-fits plot shows a clear **U-shape**: positive residuals at both ends and negative ones in the middle. What should the Green Belt do next?",options:["Add an X² term and fit a quadratic model, then re-check the residuals","Transform Y to stabilise the variance","Delete the points at both ends as outliers","Conclude that occupancy does not affect queue wait"],answer:0,hint:"Curved pattern in residuals vs fits = missing higher-order term (try X²).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A U-shaped residual pattern means the straight line is the wrong shape: it under-predicts at the ends and over-predicts in the middle."},{title:"Apply",body:"Fit Ŷ = b₀ + b₁X + b₂X², check the X² P-Value and R-sq(adj), and confirm the residuals now look random."}],final:"U-shape → add X² → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","A transformation targets a funnel (non-constant variance). A U-shape signals curvature.","The pattern is systematic across the range. Deleting points would hide a real curve.","A curved pattern shows a relationship the straight line misses, not the absence of one."]},{id:"sv-4-034",phase:4,topic:"4.2.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"A vendor proposes a **cubic** model of monthly churn because its R-sq is higher than the quadratic’s on the same data. What is the main risk of choosing the higher-order model for that reason alone?",options:["R-sq will fall when the cubic term is added","Overfitting: the extra term may track random noise, so predictions for new data get worse even though R-sq rose","A cubic model can no longer be fitted by least squares","The residuals of a cubic model are always non-normal"],answer:1,hint:"R-sq never falls when terms are added. Judge extra terms by their P-Value, R-sq(adj) and S.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Every extra term soaks up some variation, even pure noise, so R-sq always rises a little."},{title:"Apply",body:"A cubic that is not needed fits the quirks of this sample and wiggles between points, so it predicts new months badly. Check the cubic term’s P-Value and whether R-sq(adj) improves."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"R-sq cannot fall. Least squares still works. Residual normality is not forced by model order."}],final:"Main risk = overfitting → answer **B**",whyWrong:["R-sq can never fall when a term is added. That is exactly why its rise proves little.","","Polynomials are linear in their coefficients, so least squares still fits them.","The order of the model does not force the residuals to be non-normal."]},{id:"sv-4-035",phase:4,topic:"4.2.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"When comparing **linear, quadratic and cubic** fits of the same Y on the same X, which approach should decide the model order?",options:["Choose the model with the highest R-sq","Always choose the cubic, because it can follow any shape","Choose the model whose constant has the lowest P-Value","Choose the simplest model whose highest-order term is significant, with good R-sq(adj), low S and random residuals"],answer:3,hint:"Simplest adequate model: significant top term, best R-sq(adj) and S, clean residuals.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Add order step by step. Keep a higher-order term only if it is significant and improves R-sq(adj) or S."},{title:"Apply",body:"Then confirm with residual plots. Among adequate models, prefer the simplest."}],final:"Significant top term + R-sq(adj) + residuals → answer **D**",whyWrong:["R-sq always increases with order, so this would always pick the cubic.","Extra terms that are not needed overfit and make predictions unstable.","The constant’s P-Value says nothing about whether the curve shape is right.",""]},{id:"sv-4-036",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"A multiple regression of **monthly churn %** uses **p = 5 predictors** fitted to **n = 20** months and gives **R-sq = 80.0%**. What is R-sq(adj)?",options:["0.2714","0.7286","0.7467","0.8526"],answer:1,hint:"$R^2_{adj}=1-(1-R^2)\\times\\dfrac{n-1}{n-p-1}$, with p = number of predictors.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"n = 20, p = 5, R² = 0.80."},{title:"Pick the formula",body:"$$R^2_{adj}=1-(1-R^2)\\times\\dfrac{n-1}{n-p-1}$$"},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$R^2_{adj}=1-(1-0.80)\\times\\dfrac{20-1}{20-5-1}=1-0.20\\times\\dfrac{19}{14}$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$R^2_{adj}=1-0.2714=0.7286$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"R-sq(adj) ≈ 72.9% is about 7 points below R-sq. Five predictors on only 20 months carry a real penalty, so check whether all five are needed."}],final:"R-sq(adj) = 0.7286 → answer **B**",whyWrong:['The "1 −" was left off. This is only the penalty term (1 − R²) × (n − 1) ÷ (n − p − 1).',"","The constant was not counted: (n − 1) ÷ (n − p) = 19 ÷ 15 was used instead of 19 ÷ 14.","The df ratio was inverted (14 ÷ 19), which wrongly makes R-sq(adj) larger than R-sq."],trap:"R-sq(adj) can never be larger than R-sq."},{id:"sv-4-037",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"A regression of **SLA breach minutes** on **3 predictors** uses **n = 24** weeks. The ANOVA table shows **SS Regression = 360** and **SS Error = 200**. What is the F-Value for the overall model?",options:["1.80","4.93","12.00","13.80"],answer:2,hint:"$F=\\dfrac{MS_{Regression}}{MS_{Error}}$ with $MS=\\dfrac{SS}{DF}$; DF Regression = p, DF Error = n − p − 1.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"p = 3, n = 24, SS Regression = 360, SS Error = 200."},{title:"Degrees of freedom",body:"DF Regression = p = 3. DF Error = n − p − 1 = 24 − 3 − 1 = 20."},{title:"Mean squares",body:"$$MS_{Regression}=\\dfrac{360}{3}=120$$ $$MS_{Error}=\\dfrac{200}{20}=10$$"},{title:"Calculate F",body:"$$F=\\dfrac{MS_{Regression}}{MS_{Error}}=\\dfrac{120}{10}=12.00$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"F = 12 on (3, 20) DF is well above the 5% critical value (about 3.10). At least one predictor is significant."}],final:"F = 12.00 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The sums of squares were divided (360 ÷ 200) instead of the mean squares.","MS Regression was divided by the total mean square (560 ÷ 23) instead of MS Error.","","MS Error used n − 1 = 23 df (200 ÷ 23) instead of the Error DF n − p − 1 = 20."]},{id:"sv-4-038",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"A multiple regression of **fibre-install time** has **4 predictors** and is fitted to **n = 30** jobs. What are the **Regression DF** and **Error DF** in its ANOVA table?",options:["Regression DF = 3, Error DF = 26","Regression DF = 4, Error DF = 25","Regression DF = 4, Error DF = 26","Regression DF = 5, Error DF = 24"],answer:1,hint:"DF Regression = p; DF Error = n − p − 1; DF Total = n − 1.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"p = 4 predictors, n = 30 observations, plus a constant."},{title:"Apply the DF rules",body:"DF Regression = p = **4**. DF Error = n − p − 1 = 30 − 4 − 1 = **25**. DF Total = n − 1 = 29, and 4 + 25 = 29 ✓."},{title:"Why it matters",body:"These DF set the mean squares: $$MS_{Error}=\\dfrac{SS_{Error}}{25}$$ and they fix the F and t critical values."}],final:"DF = 4 and 25 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The predictors were treated like ANOVA factor levels (p − 1). In regression each predictor uses one DF.","","The DF for the constant was not subtracted (30 − 4).","The constant was counted as a predictor in the Regression DF."]},{id:"sv-4-039",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:2,stem:"In a multiple regression of **ticket resolution time** (n = 30, 3 predictors), the term **Automation%** has **Coef = 0.84** and **SE Coef = 0.35**. What is its T-Value?",options:["0.29","0.42","2.40","6.86"],answer:2,hint:"$t=\\dfrac{Coef}{SE\\ Coef}$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Coef = 0.84, SE Coef = 0.35; Error DF = 30 − 3 − 1 = 26."},{title:"Pick the formula and substitute",body:"$$t=\\dfrac{Coef}{SE\\ Coef}=\\dfrac{0.84}{0.35}=2.40$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"|t| = 2.40 > t₀.₀₂₅,₂₆ ≈ 2.056 (P ≈ 0.024), so Automation% is significant at α = 0.05."}],final:"t = 2.40 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The coefficient was multiplied by its standard error (0.84 × 0.35) instead of divided.","The ratio was inverted (0.35 ÷ 0.84).","","The coefficient was divided by SE² (0.35² = 0.1225), treating the SE like a variance."]},{id:"sv-4-040",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"To check multicollinearity, the predictor **Backlog** is regressed on the other X’s in the model, giving **R²ⱼ = 0.88**. What is the VIF for Backlog?",options:["0.12","1.14","4.43","8.33"],answer:3,hint:"$VIF_j=\\dfrac{1}{1-R_j^2}$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"R²ⱼ = 0.88: the other X’s explain 88% of Backlog’s variation."},{title:"Pick the formula and substitute",body:"$$VIF=\\dfrac{1}{1-R_j^2}=\\dfrac{1}{1-0.88}=\\dfrac{1}{0.12}=8.33$$"},{title:"Decide",body:"VIF = 8.33 is above the common guideline of 5, a sign of strong multicollinearity. The variance of Backlog’s coefficient is inflated about 8 times."}],final:"VIF = 8.33 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["This is 1 − R²ⱼ, the denominator, not its reciprocal.","R²ⱼ was used in the denominator (1 ÷ 0.88) instead of 1 − R²ⱼ.","R²ⱼ was squared (1 ÷ (1 − 0.88²)). R²ⱼ is already a squared quantity.",""]},{id:"sv-4-041",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:3,stem:"Minitab reports **VIF = 4.0** for the predictor **Hold time** in a handle-time model. What is R²ⱼ, the R² from regressing Hold time on the other predictors?",options:["0.25","0.50","0.75","0.94"],answer:2,hint:"$VIF=\\dfrac{1}{1-R_j^2}\\;\\Rightarrow\\;R_j^2=1-\\dfrac{1}{VIF}$",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"VIF = 4.0."},{title:"Rearrange the formula",body:"$$VIF=\\dfrac{1}{1-R_j^2}\\Rightarrow 1-R_j^2=\\dfrac{1}{VIF}\\Rightarrow R_j^2=1-\\dfrac{1}{VIF}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$R_j^2=1-\\dfrac{1}{4}=1-0.25=0.75$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"75% of Hold time’s variation is explained by the other X’s. VIF = 4 is below the usual concern level of 5, so it is moderate."}],final:"R²ⱼ = 0.75 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["This is 1 ÷ VIF = 1 − R²ⱼ, the unexplained share, not R²ⱼ.","√VIF = 2 was used in place of VIF (1 − 1 ÷ 2).","","VIF was squared (1 − 1 ÷ 16)."]},{id:"sv-4-042",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:`A P2 **restore-time (h)** model is

**Ŷ = 2.5 + 0.8 Alarms − 1.2 Engineers + 0.05 Distance_km**

Predict restore time for **10 alarms, 3 engineers and 40 km** (all inside the data range).`,options:["6.40 h","6.90 h","8.90 h","16.10 h"],answer:2,hint:"$\\hat{Y}=b_0+b_1X_1+b_2X_2+b_3X_3$ — include every term with its own sign.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"b₀ = 2.5; Alarms = 10 (b = 0.8); Engineers = 3 (b = −1.2); Distance = 40 (b = 0.05)."},{title:"Substitute",body:"$$\\hat{Y}=2.5+0.8\\times 10-1.2\\times 3+0.05\\times 40$$"},{title:"Calculate",body:"$$\\hat{Y}=2.5+8.0-3.6+2.0=8.9$$"},{title:"Interpret",body:"Each coefficient is the change in Y per unit of that X **with the other X’s held constant**. Each extra engineer cuts about 1.2 h."}],final:"Ŷ = 8.90 h → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The constant 2.5 was left out.","The Distance term (0.05 × 40 = 2.0) was left out.","","The minus sign on the Engineers term was ignored (+3.6 instead of −3.6)."]},{id:"sv-4-043",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Minitab output for **provisioning time (h)** (n = 30):

\`\`\`
Regression Analysis: Provision_h versus Lines, Sites, Approvals

Coefficients
Term        Coef  SE Coef  T-Value  P-Value    VIF
Constant    3.10     1.20     2.58    0.016
Lines      0.412    0.390     1.06    0.300  11.80
Sites      0.950    0.880     1.08    0.290  12.40
Approvals  1.380    0.310     4.45    0.000   1.10

Model Summary
      S    R-sq  R-sq(adj)
   2.05  81.2%     79.0%
\`\`\`

What is the MOST appropriate next step?`,options:["Remove both Lines and Sites, because both P-Values are above 0.05","Keep all terms, because R-sq = 81.2% shows the model is good","Address the multicollinearity: remove one of Lines or Sites, refit, and re-check the P-Values and VIFs","Remove Approvals, because it has the lowest VIF"],answer:2,hint:"VIF > 5–10 = multicollinearity. Remove one of the correlated X’s at a time and refit.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"VIF: Lines 11.80 and Sites 12.40, both above 10. Approvals 1.10. P-Values: Lines 0.300 and Sites 0.290, both non-significant."},{title:"Diagnose",body:"Lines and Sites are strongly correlated with each other. Each one’s SE Coef is inflated, which hides their joint effect. This is why a model with R-sq 81% can have two non-significant terms."},{title:"Act",body:"Drop one of the pair (based on process knowledge or the higher P-Value), refit, and the other will often become significant. Then re-check the VIFs and residuals."}],final:"High VIFs → remove one collinear X and refit → answer **C**",whyWrong:["With VIFs near 12, Lines and Sites share information and inflate each other’s SE. Removing both could throw away a real effect.","A high R-sq does not fix multicollinearity. The coefficients of Lines and Sites are unstable and cannot be trusted.","","A low VIF is good. Approvals is the clearest, most significant predictor."],trap:"Never delete several terms at once on P-Values alone. Remove one at a time."},{id:"sv-4-044",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Minitab output for **average handle time (s)** (n = 28, α = 0.05):

\`\`\`
Regression Analysis: AHT_s versus Systems, Tenure_m, Hold_s

Coefficients
Term        Coef  SE Coef  T-Value  P-Value   VIF
Constant   148.2      9.6    15.44    0.000
Systems    11.40     2.10     5.43    0.000  1.20
Tenure_m   -0.62     0.21    -2.95    0.007  1.30
Hold_s      0.08     0.11     0.73    0.474  1.10

Model Summary
      S    R-sq  R-sq(adj)
   14.3  72.4%     69.0%
\`\`\`

Which action is MOST appropriate?`,options:["Remove Tenure_m, because its coefficient is negative","Conclude that hold time has no effect on AHT in any situation","Remove Systems, because its large T-Value shows it dominates the other predictors","Remove Hold_s (P = 0.474) and refit the model with Systems and Tenure_m"],answer:3,hint:"Remove the term with the highest P-Value above α (one at a time) and refit. VIFs near 1 mean no multicollinearity.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"P-Values: Systems 0.000, Tenure_m 0.007, Hold_s **0.474**. All VIFs are about 1, so the P-Values are reliable."},{title:"Decide",body:"Hold_s: 0.474 > 0.05, so fail to reject H₀: β = 0. Remove it and refit."},{title:"After refitting",body:"Check that R-sq(adj) holds or improves and that the residual plots are clean."}],final:"Hold_s P = 0.474 > 0.05 → remove it → answer **D**",whyWrong:["A negative coefficient only means AHT falls as tenure rises. P = 0.007 shows it is significant.","A P-Value above α means fail to reject. It does not prove there is no effect, especially outside these data.","A large |T| means strong evidence the term matters. It is a reason to keep it.",""]},{id:"sv-4-045",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Best Subsets output for **monthly churn %** (n = 30; candidates Price, Outages, Tickets, Tenure):

\`\`\`
Best Subsets Regression: Churn_pct

Vars  R-Sq  R-Sq(adj)  Mallows Cp      S   Predictors
   1  61.3      60.0        24.9  1.439   Price
   1  50.0      48.2        39.8  1.637   Tickets
   2  76.7      74.9         6.7  1.139   Price, Tickets
   2  73.3      71.4        11.1  1.217   Price, Outages
   3  80.7      78.5         3.4  1.054   Price, Outages, Tickets
   3  78.0      75.5         6.9  1.127   Price, Tickets, Tenure
   4  81.0      78.0         5.0  1.068   Price, Outages, Tickets, Tenure
\`\`\`

Which model is the BEST choice?`,options:["Price, Outages, Tickets, because it has the highest R-Sq(adj), the lowest S and Mallows Cp close to 4 (vars + 1)","Price only, because it is the simplest model","Price, Tickets, because it reaches high R-Sq(adj) with only two predictors","All four predictors, because it has the highest R-Sq"],answer:0,hint:"Best subsets: high R-Sq(adj), low S, Mallows Cp close to vars + 1, fewest predictors.",steps:[{title:"Compare R-Sq(adj) and S",body:"The 3-variable model Price, Outages, Tickets has the highest R-Sq(adj) (78.5%) and the lowest S (1.054)."},{title:"Check Mallows Cp",body:"Its Cp is 3.4, close to vars + 1 = 4, so it is not biased. The 2-variable models have Cp well above 3."},{title:"Choose",body:"The 4-variable model has the highest R-Sq but a lower R-Sq(adj) and larger S. Pick Price, Outages, Tickets, then check its P-Values, VIFs and residuals."}],final:"Price, Outages, Tickets → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","One predictor leaves R-Sq(adj) at 60.0% and Mallows Cp at 24.9, far above 2. The model is missing important X’s.","Its Mallows Cp of 6.7 is well above 3 (vars + 1), which signals bias from a missing term.","R-Sq always rises with more terms. Adding Tenure lowers R-Sq(adj) (78.0%) and raises S, so it adds nothing."],trap:"R-Sq picks the biggest model every time. Use R-Sq(adj), S and Cp."},{id:"sv-4-046",phase:4,topic:"4.2.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All of the following statements about **R-sq(adj)** in multiple regression are true **EXCEPT**:",options:["It penalises predictors that do not improve the model enough","It always increases when another predictor is added","It is used to compare models with different numbers of predictors","It is never larger than R-sq"],answer:1,hint:"$R^2_{adj}=1-(1-R^2)\\times\\dfrac{n-1}{n-p-1}$ — it can fall when a weak predictor is added.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"R-sq never falls when a term is added. R-sq(adj) adds a penalty for each predictor, so it rises only if the new term explains enough."},{title:"Apply",body:'The statement "always increases" describes R-sq, not R-sq(adj).'}],final:'"Always increases" is false for R-sq(adj) → answer **B**',whyWrong:["This is true. The (n − 1) ÷ (n − p − 1) factor grows as p increases.","","This is true. It is the main reason R-sq(adj) is reported.","This is true. The penalty factor is at least 1, so R-sq(adj) ≤ R-sq."]},{id:"sv-4-047",phase:4,topic:"4.2.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Minitab prediction for **P2 restore time (h)** at the planned settings:

\`\`\`
Prediction for Restore_h
Restore_h = 9.20 - 0.350 Engineers + 0.0410 Alarms

Settings: Engineers = 6, Alarms = 40

   Fit   SE Fit         95% CI            95% PI
 8.740    0.212   (8.304, 9.176)   (6.457, 11.023)
\`\`\`

The service manager must quote a restore time that will cover **the next single P2 incident** with these settings, with 95% confidence. Which interval should be quoted?`,options:["(8.304, 9.176) h","(8.528, 8.952) h","(6.457, 11.023) h","Exactly 8.740 h"],answer:2,hint:"One future observation → prediction interval (PI). Average response → confidence interval (CI).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The PI adds the scatter of individual values (S) to the uncertainty in the fitted mean, so it covers a single new observation."},{title:"Apply",body:"The question is about **the next single incident**, so use the 95% PI: **6.457 to 11.023 h**."},{title:"Eliminate",body:"The CI is for the mean. Fit ± SE Fit is neither 95% nor for an individual. The fit alone has no uncertainty attached."}],final:"95% PI (6.457, 11.023) h → answer **C**",whyWrong:["This is the CI for the **mean** restore time of all such incidents. It ignores incident-to-incident scatter.","This is Fit ± one SE Fit. It is not a 95% interval and it describes the mean, not a single incident.","","The fit is a point estimate. A single incident will almost never take exactly this time."]},{id:"sv-4-048",phase:4,topic:"4.2.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:'The order-provisioning SLA is **48 h**. At the planned staffing, a regression gives a **95% CI for the mean of (40.2, 43.8) h** and a **95% PI of (33.5, 50.5) h**. A manager says, "The model proves every order will meet the 48-hour SLA." What is the correct assessment?',options:["Correct, because the CI upper limit of 43.8 h is below 48 h","Wrong: no order will meet the SLA, because the PI includes 48 h","The SLA cannot be judged until R-sq is above 90%","Wrong: the average order should meet the SLA, but some individual orders may exceed 48 h, because the PI reaches 50.5 h"],answer:3,hint:"CI → where the mean will be. PI → where individual values will fall.",steps:[{title:"Read the CI",body:"Mean provisioning time is likely between 40.2 and 43.8 h, below 48 h. **On average** the SLA is met."},{title:"Read the PI",body:"Individual orders are expected between 33.5 and 50.5 h. The upper part (48–50.5 h) breaches the SLA."},{title:"Conclude",body:"The claim mixes up the two intervals. To protect every order, the process needs less scatter or more capacity."}],final:"Mean OK, individuals can breach → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The CI describes the average order. Individual orders scatter much more widely.","Most of the PI lies below 48 h, so most orders are expected to meet the SLA.","There is no such rule. The intervals already carry the uncertainty needed for the judgement.",""]},{id:"sv-4-049",phase:4,topic:"4.2.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"On a fitted line plot with 95% CI and PI bands, why do the bands get **wider towards the ends** of the X range?",options:["Because the uncertainty in the fitted line grows with the distance of X from x̄","Because the residuals are always larger at the ends of the data","Because fewer predictors are used at the ends of the range","Because S is calculated separately for each X value"],answer:0,hint:"Both intervals contain the term $\\dfrac{(x_0-\\bar{x})^2}{S_{xx}}$, which grows as $x_0$ moves away from $\\bar{x}$.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The SE of the fit is $$S\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n}+\\dfrac{(x_0-\\bar{x})^2}{S_{xx}}}$$ It is smallest at $x_0=\\bar{x}$."},{title:"Apply",body:"A small error in the slope moves the line more the further you go from the centre (x̄, ȳ), like a see-saw. So the bands flare outward."}],final:"Distance from x̄ widens the bands → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","The model assumes constant residual variance. The widening comes from the line, not the residuals.","The same model and predictors apply at every X.","S is one value for the whole model."]},{id:"sv-4-050",phase:4,topic:"4.2.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"At the same X value and confidence level, the 95% **confidence interval** for the mean response is **wider** than the 95% **prediction interval** for a single new observation. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"PI = uncertainty of the mean + scatter of individuals, so PI > CI.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The CI half-width uses $$S\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n}+\\dfrac{(x_0-\\bar{x})^2}{S_{xx}}}$$ while the PI adds 1 inside the root: $$S\\sqrt{1+\\dfrac{1}{n}+\\dfrac{(x_0-\\bar{x})^2}{S_{xx}}}$$"},{title:"Conclude",body:"The extra 1 makes the PI wider at every X, so the statement is false."}],final:"CI is narrower than PI → **False** (answer **B**)",whyWrong:["It is the other way round. The PI adds the scatter of individual values, so it is always wider than the CI.",""]},{id:"sv-4-051",phase:4,topic:"4.2.3",qtype:"table",difficulty:3,stem:`A model of **average queue wait (s)** on **agents staffed (X)** was fitted to 20 intervals. Minitab’s predictions at four settings:

| X (agents) | Fit | 95% CI | 95% PI |
|---:|---:|---|---|
| 8 | 62.1 | (59.1, 65.1) | (53.4, 70.8) |
| 14 | 48.0 | (46.1, 49.9) | (39.6, 56.4) |
| 20 | 33.9 | (31.4, 36.4) | (25.3, 42.5) |
| 26 | 19.8 | (15.7, 23.9) | (10.6, 29.0) |

Which setting is closest to the **mean X (x̄)** of the data used to fit the model?`,options:["X = 8","X = 20","X = 14","X = 26"],answer:2,hint:"The CI half-width is smallest when $x_0=\\bar{x}$ and grows with $(x_0-\\bar{x})^2$.",steps:[{title:"Compute the CI widths",body:"X = 8: 65.1 − 59.1 = 6.0. X = 14: 49.9 − 46.1 = 3.8. X = 20: 36.4 − 31.4 = 5.0. X = 26: 23.9 − 15.7 = 8.2."},{title:"Apply the rule",body:"The narrowest CI (3.8 s) is at X = 14, so X = 14 is closest to x̄. The PI widths show the same order."},{title:"Interpret",body:"Predictions are most precise near the centre of the data and least precise at the edges."}],final:"Narrowest CI at X = 14 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Its CI is 6.0 s wide, wider than at X = 14, so it is further from x̄.","20 looks central in the table, but its CI (5.0 s wide) is wider than at X = 14.","","This setting has the **widest** CI (8.2 s), so it is the furthest from x̄."]},{id:"sv-4-052",phase:4,topic:"4.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A two-predictor model of **daily ticket backlog** uses 50 days of data in time order. The **residuals-versus-order** plot shows residuals scattered around zero for days 1–25, then almost all **below zero from day 26 onward**. Day 26 was when a new ticketing tool went live. What should the team do?",options:["Transform Y with Box-Cox to fix the non-normal residuals","Add squared terms for both predictors to capture curvature","Delete days 26–50 as outliers and refit on days 1–25","Treat it as a missing time-related X: add a before/after-tool indicator variable (or model the periods separately) and refit"],answer:3,hint:"A step or trend in residuals vs order = residuals not independent; something time-related is missing from the model.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Residuals versus order checks independence. A sustained step means the model is missing a factor that changed at that point."},{title:"Apply",body:"The new tool lowered backlog beyond what the two X’s explain. Add an indicator X (0 before, 1 after) and refit. Its coefficient estimates the tool’s effect."}],final:"Step in order plot → add the missing time-related X → answer **D**",whyWrong:["The problem is a shift over time (dependence), not the shape of the distribution.","Curvature shows in residuals versus fits or versus X, not as a step in time order.","Half the data cannot be outliers. Deleting them would also throw away the effect of the new tool.",""]},{id:"sv-4-053",phase:4,topic:"4.2.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"In a 3-predictor model fitted to 40 incidents, Minitab’s **Fits and Diagnostics for Unusual Observations** lists **Obs 17** marked **X** (unusual X values, Std Resid 0.4) and **Obs 23** marked **R** (Std Resid 2.9). What is the BEST next step?",options:["Investigate both for data errors or special causes, and refit with and without them to see how much they change the model","Delete both observations and refit, because unusual points always distort a model","Ignore Obs 17, because its residual is small, and delete Obs 23","Report the model as invalid and start again with new X’s"],answer:0,hint:"R = large standardized residual; X = high leverage (unusual X). Investigate the cause before removing anything.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"An R point is poorly fitted. An X point has unusual predictor values and can be influential. Neither is automatically an error."},{title:"Apply",body:"Check the source records for both. Fit the model with and without them. If the coefficients change a lot, the points are influential. Remove a point only if there is a documented cause, such as a data-entry error."}],final:"Investigate, compare fits, remove only with a cause → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Unusual points can be valid and informative. Deleting them without a cause biases the model.","A high-leverage point can pull the line towards itself, which is why its residual looks small. A large residual is not proof of bad data.","Two unusual observations out of 40 do not invalidate a model. They need investigation first."]},{id:"sv-4-054",phase:4,topic:"4.2.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"In a multiple regression, the plot of residuals against the predictor **X₂** shows a clear **curved** pattern, while the other residual plots look fine. What does this MOST likely indicate?",options:["Multicollinearity between X₂ and another predictor","The relationship between Y and X₂ is curved; add an X₂² term or transform X₂","The residuals are not normally distributed","The residuals are autocorrelated over time"],answer:1,hint:"Residuals vs a predictor: a curve means that predictor needs a non-linear term.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Plotting residuals against each X shows whether that X enters the model in the right form."},{title:"Apply",body:"A curve against X₂ means a straight-line term in X₂ is not enough. Add X₂² (or transform X₂), refit, and check the new term’s P-Value and R-sq(adj)."}],final:"Curve vs X₂ → add X₂² → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Multicollinearity is detected with VIFs. It does not create curvature in a residual plot.","","Normality is judged on the normal probability plot, which looks fine here.","Autocorrelation shows in the residuals-versus-order plot, not in residuals against a predictor."]},{id:"sv-4-055",phase:4,topic:"4.2.4",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put these multiple-regression steps in the correct order:

- **P** — Fit the full model with all candidate X’s
- **Q** — Check the VIFs for multicollinearity
- **R** — Remove non-significant terms one at a time (highest P-Value first) and refit
- **S** — Analyse the residuals of the reduced model before using it`,options:["P → R → Q → S","S → P → Q → R","P → Q → R → S","P → Q → S → R"],answer:2,hint:"Fit → check VIF → reduce one term at a time → validate the final model’s residuals.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Multicollinearity distorts P-Values, so check VIFs before trusting them to prune."},{title:"Apply",body:"P: fit all X’s. Q: resolve high VIFs. R: drop the least significant term, refit, repeat. S: validate the final model with residual plots."}],final:"P → Q → R → S → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Pruning before checking VIFs can drop a useful X whose P-Value is only inflated by multicollinearity.","Residuals cannot be analysed before a model is fitted.","","Residuals are checked on a model that then changes. The final, reduced model must be the one validated."]},{id:"sv-4-056",phase:4,topic:"4.2.5",qtype:"calculation",difficulty:1,stem:"Box-Cox recommends **λ = −0.5** for a set of positive **ticket-handling times (min)**. What is the transformed value of a time of **25 min**?",options:["−12.50","0.04","0.20","5.00"],answer:2,hint:"Box-Cox: transformed value $=Y^{\\lambda}$ (λ = 0 means ln Y). $Y^{-0.5}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{Y}}$.",steps:[{title:"Write what is given",body:"Y = 25, λ = −0.5."},{title:"Pick the transform",body:"$$Y^{\\lambda}=Y^{-0.5}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{Y}}$$"},{title:"Substitute and calculate",body:"$$Y^{-0.5}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{25}}=\\dfrac{1}{5}=0.20$$"},{title:"Remember",body:"λ = −0.5 is the reciprocal square root. Apply the same transform to the specification limits."}],final:"Y′ = 0.20 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["The value was multiplied by λ (25 × −0.5) instead of raised to the power λ.","This is 1 ÷ 25, the λ = −1 (reciprocal) transform.","","This is √25, the λ = +0.5 transform. The minus sign of λ was dropped."]},{id:"sv-4-057",phase:4,topic:"4.2.5",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Minitab’s Box-Cox analysis gives a **rounded λ = 0**. Which transformation should be applied to the data?",options:["Natural logarithm, ln Y","No transformation","Square root, √Y","Reciprocal, $\\dfrac{1}{Y}$"],answer:0,hint:"λ: 2 → Y², 1 → none, 0.5 → √Y, 0 → ln Y, −0.5 → $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{Y}}$, −1 → $\\dfrac{1}{Y}$.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Y⁰ would turn every value into 1, which is useless. So Box-Cox defines λ = 0 as the natural log."},{title:"Apply",body:"Take ln of every observation and of the specification limits, then re-check normality."}],final:"λ = 0 → ln Y → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","No transformation corresponds to λ = 1.","The square root corresponds to λ = 0.5.","The reciprocal corresponds to λ = −1."]},{id:"sv-4-058",phase:4,topic:"4.2.5",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"Order-to-activate cycle times are strongly **right-skewed** (Anderson-Darling p < 0.005). All values are positive, and the team has confirmed there are no data errors or mixed populations. They need a capability estimate against **USL = 72 h**. What is the BEST approach?",options:["Delete the longest cycle times as outliers until the data pass the normality test","Apply a Box-Cox transformation to the data and the USL, then run capability on the transformed scale","Run a normal capability analysis anyway, because Cpk is robust to skewness","Transform the data with Box-Cox but keep the USL at 72 h"],answer:1,hint:"Positive, skewed, clean data → Box-Cox (transform the specification limits too) or a non-normal capability analysis.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Box-Cox finds the power λ that makes the data closest to normal. It needs positive data and is used after the causes of non-normality have been checked."},{title:"Apply",body:"The causes are checked and the data are positive. Transform the data **and** the USL with the same λ, confirm normality, then run capability."}],final:"Box-Cox data and USL → answer **B**",whyWrong:["The long times are real and confirmed. Deleting them would hide exactly the orders that breach the USL.","","Normal-based capability is sensitive to skew and misstates the tail beyond the USL.","The limit must be on the same scale as the data. Comparing transformed data with an untransformed USL is meaningless."]},{id:"sv-4-059",phase:4,topic:"4.2.5",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Each of the following statements about the **Box-Cox transformation** is correct **EXCEPT**:",options:["It searches for the power λ that makes the data closest to a normal distribution","If the confidence interval for λ includes 1, a transformation is usually not needed","Specification limits must be transformed with the same λ as the data","It can be applied directly to data containing zeros and negative values"],answer:3,hint:"Box-Cox needs strictly positive data (Y > 0).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Powers and logs of zero or negative numbers are undefined or flip the order (for example ln 0, or $\\dfrac{1}{Y}$ for negative Y)."},{title:"Apply",body:"Data with zeros or negatives need a shift (add a constant) or another method, such as a Johnson transformation, first."}],final:"Box-Cox needs positive data → answer **D**",whyWrong:["This is correct. It is what the Box-Cox plot does.","This is correct. λ = 1 leaves the data unchanged.","This is correct. Otherwise data and limits are on different scales.",""]},{id:"sv-4-060",phase:4,topic:"4.2.5",qtype:"table",difficulty:3,stem:`Minitab’s Box-Cox output for 60 **dispute-resolution times (days)**:

| Estimate λ | Lower CL | Upper CL | Rounded value |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 0.08 | −0.21 | 0.39 | 0.00 |

What should the analyst do?`,options:["Use no transformation, because the interval contains 0","Use the square root, because the upper limit 0.39 is closest to 0.5","Use the reciprocal, because the lower limit is negative","Apply the natural log transformation (λ = 0)"],answer:3,hint:"Use the rounded λ. If the CI for λ excludes 1, transform. λ = 0 means ln Y.",steps:[{title:"Locate",body:"Rounded λ = **0.00**. CI for λ: −0.21 to 0.39."},{title:"Decide whether to transform",body:"1 is **not** inside (−0.21, 0.39), so the untransformed data are not adequate. A transform is needed."},{title:"Choose the transform",body:"λ = 0 is inside the interval and is the rounded value, so use ln Y. Apply ln to the specification limits as well."}],final:"λ = 0 → ln transform → answer **D**",whyWrong:['The test for "no transformation needed" is whether the interval contains **1**. This interval stops at 0.39.',"The recommended value is the rounded λ inside the interval, 0, not the nearest convenient value outside it.","A negative lower limit does not point to λ = −1. The reciprocal lies well outside the interval.",""]}];export{e as items};
